Nonreciprocity, defined as the asymmetric transmission of light between two ports, is a critical element for numerous photonic applications. While quantum squeezing can enhance the sensitivity of optical measurements, its impact on nonreciprocity remains unexplored. This study demonstrates that quantum squeezing can induce nonreciprocity in a linear, reciprocal, and time-invariant system. Specifically, we show that a two-port system comprising two coupled waveguides with a single point defect can exhibit nonreciprocal behavior when driven by a squeezed vacuum field. Furthermore, we establish that the degree of nonreciprocity is directly proportional to the level of quantum squeezing. These findings offer a novel approach to achieving nonreciprocity in linear systems and may have significant implications for quantum information processing. Additionally, we provide a comprehensive analysis of the underlying physical mechanisms. Finally, we outline potential applications of this phenomenon in quantum information processing and quantum communication. Our results are validated through numerical simulations. The source code for these simulations is available at https://github.com/ChenYi2019/Nonreciprocity. This work was supported by the National Natural Science Foundation of China (Grant No. 11974270) and the Fundamental Research Funds for the Central Universities (Grant No. 20720201017). Correspondence: Y. Chen (chenyi@hust.edu.cn) and Y. Li (yili@hust.edu.cn). Manuscript received 2020-09-01; revised 2020-10-15. Published 2020-10-20. DOI: 10.1109/ACCESS.2020.3030200. Index Terms: Quantum squeezing, nonreciprocity, quantum information processing, quantum communication.
